Step 1: Create the main project
1. Open Visual Studio.
2. Click Create a new project.
3. Search Class Library.
4. Select Class Library → Next.
5. Project name:
CalculatorApp
6. Select the framework available in your college, e.g. .NET 8.0.
7. Click Create.
Step 2: Create the testing project
In Solution Explorer:
1. Right-click the Solution.
2. Select:
Add → New Project
3. Search:
xUnit
4. Select xUnit Test Project.
5. Project name:
CalculatorApp.Tests
6. Click Create. Your solution should look like:
Solution
│
├── CalculatorApp
│ └── Class1.cs
│
└── CalculatorApp.Tests
└── UnitTest1.cs
Step 3: Add project reference
The test project needs access to CalculatorApp.
1. Right-click:
CalculatorApp.Tests
2. Select Add → Project Reference.
3. Tick:
CalculatorApp
4. Click OK.
Now the test project can use the Calculator class.
Step 4: Create Calculator class
Inside:
CalculatorApp
Right-click → Add → Class
Name: Calculator.cs
Replace the code with:
namespace CalculatorApp;
public class Calculator
{
public int Add(int a, int b)
{
return a + b;
}
public int Subtract(int a, int b)
{
return a - b;
}
}
Save with Ctrl + S.
Step 5: Write test cases
Open:
CalculatorApp.Tests
└── UnitTest1.cs
Replace everything with:
using CalculatorApp;
namespace CalculatorApp.Tests;
public class UnitTest1 {
[Fact]
public void Add_TwoNumbers_ReturnsCorrectResult()
{
Calculator calculator = new Calculator();
int result = calculator.Add(10, 20);
Assert.Equal(30, result);
}
[Fact]
public void Subtract_TwoNumbers_ReturnsCorrectResult()
{
Calculator calculator = new Calculator();
int result = calculator.Subtract(20, 10);
Assert.Equal(10, result);
}
}
Save.
Step 6: Run the tests
Go to:
Test → Test Explorer
Then click:
Run All Tests You should see:
Passed: 2
Failed: 0
Skipped: 0
Green checkmarks mean the tests passed.
